## TEC 29.003 - Eligibility Criteria

(a) The agency shall develop specific eligibility criteria based on the general classifications established by this section with reference to contemporary diagnostic or evaluative terminology and methodology.
(b) A student is eligible to participate in a school district's special education program if the student:
(1) is not more than 21 years of age and has a visual or auditory impairment that prevents the student from being adequately or safely educated in public school without the provision of special services; or
(2) is at least three years of age and not more than 21 years of age and has one or more of the following disabilities that prevents the student from being adequately or safely educated in public school without the provision of special services:
(A) intellectual disability;
(B) a speech or language impairment;
(C) an emotional disturbance;
(D) a learning disability;
(E) autism;
(F) traumatic brain injury;
(G) a visual or auditory impairment;
(H) an orthopedic impairment;
(I) other health impairment;
(J) deaf-blindness; or
(K) multiple disabilities.

## TEC 29.005 - Individualized Education Program (IEP / ARD)

(a) Before a child is placed in a special education program, a committee composed of the persons described in Section 29.005(b) shall develop the child's individualized education program (referred to in Texas as the ARD committee).
(b) The committee shall include:
(1) the person or persons best qualified to interpret the evaluation data;
(2) a regular education teacher if the child is, or may be, participating in the regular education environment;
(3) a special education teacher;
(4) a representative of the school district;
(5) the child's parent or legal guardian;
(6) the child, if appropriate; and
(7) other individuals at the discretion of the parent or the district.

## TEC 29.0051 - Information and Consent for Certain Psychological Examinations or Tests

Before obtaining the consent of a parent for a full individual and initial evaluation of a student, the school district must provide the parent with:
(1) information describing the evaluation process;
(2) information listing the evaluation measures that may be used;
(3) information about the differences between an individualized education program under IDEA and a Section 504 plan.

